# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Pages response headers
#
# Avoid overlapping Cache-Control rules: deployed Pages responses can combine
# matching values instead of replacing them, yielding conflicting directives.
# Keep the feed/asset Cache-Control rules specific; rely on Pages' default for
# other HTML responses.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vite emits content-hashed filenames (index-BAZjQAaJ.js). The hash changes
# whenever the bytes change, so the file at a given URL is immutable forever.
# Without this rule Pages falls back to `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which
# forces a revalidation round-trip for the JS/CSS bundle on EVERY repeat visit
# — directly inflating FCP and LCP for returning users.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Brand SVG marks are versioned by filename (`-v1`), so they are as immutable
# as a hashed bundle. They used to inherit the 7-day brand rule below, which
# Lighthouse's "efficient cache lifetimes" audit flags for anything under 30
# days. Bump the `-vN` suffix when a mark changes.
/assets/brand/svg/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Favicons are the deliberate counter-example: they are read from a browser
# favicon cache that does not reliably revalidate, so a year-long TTL is how a
# rebrand becomes invisible to returning visitors. Keep them short and allow a
# stale copy to be served while revalidating in the background.
/assets/brand/favicon/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=2592000

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

# Self-hosted fonts (Phase 1). The original 7-day rule fixed the real bug it
# was written for — Pages' default `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, i.e. a
# revalidation round-trip on every repeat visit for the two preloaded files.
# But 7 days still fails Lighthouse's "efficient cache lifetimes" audit, which
# flags anything under 30 days — it looks for a ~92.5% cache-hit probability on
# repeat visits and estimated 28 KiB of wasted transfer on this site (the panel
# lists all 266 KiB of font transfer it inspected; the estimate is
# probability-weighted, not the listed total).
#
# The files are NOT content-hashed, so they are versioned by filename instead
# (`inter-400-v1.woff2`). Before editing a font file: bump the `-vN` suffix and
# update every reference. `npm run verify:build` fails if a referenced font is
# unversioned or missing, so a silent stale-font deploy is not possible.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Crawlable feeds: short TTL so sitemap/RSS updates reach crawlers quickly.
/sitemap.xml
  Content-Type: application/xml; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/rss.xml
  Content-Type: application/xml; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/robots.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Prerendered HTML must stay fresh (content changes without a URL change).
# Cloudflare Pages defaults static HTML to revalidation; don't add a second,
# broad Cache-Control value that would conflict with the specific rules above.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Content-Security-Policy: object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security headers — what is here, and what is deliberately NOT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lighthouse files csp-xss, has-hsts, origin-isolation, clickjacking-mitigation
# and trusted-types-xss under "Trust and Safety". All five are INFORMATIVE
# audits: they cannot move the Best Practices score. They were added because
# they are cheap and real, in this order of risk:
#
# - HSTS: this host is HTTPS-only on Cloudflare Pages, so pinning it costs
#   nothing. `preload` is required by the Lighthouse audit (it fails on a
#   missing preload directive); submitting the domain to hstspreload.org is a
#   separate, deliberate decision — the directive alone does nothing.
# - X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN + CSP `frame-ancestors 'self'`: clickjacking
#   protection. Sent together for old browsers. ⚠️ If /travelpayouts-wl.html
#   ever has to be embedded cross-origin, this rule must be scoped to explicit
#   paths instead of `/*` — Cloudflare merges matching rules and cannot
#   unset a header per path.
# - Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: `same-origin-allow-popups`, not `same-origin`,
#   because the Travelpayouts/Emerald widgets open cross-origin popups and
#   severing window.opener would break their handoff. Any of the four allowed
#   COOP values satisfies the audit; this is the one that keeps popups working.
# - CSP is deliberately PARTIAL: object-src/base-uri/frame-ancestors only. A
#   real XSS-mitigating `script-src` needs nonces or hashes for the four inline
#   scripts in this shell (consent default, GTM loader, emrld loader, JSON-LD)
#   plus allowlists for googletagmanager.com, emrld.ltd and the Travelpayouts
#   widget bundles. That is a project with its own testing matrix, not a header
#   line — see Appendix D.3.
#
# NOT added, on purpose:
# - Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy (COEP): `require-corp` would block every
#   third-party asset that does not send CORP/CORS headers — the fonts are
#   same-origin, but the affiliate widgets, GTM and the review embeds are not.
#   COEP without cooperation from all of them breaks the page.
# - `require-trusted-types-for 'script'`: React 19 does not create a Trusted
#   Types policy, and the affiliate widgets inject script/markup directly.
#   Enabling it blanks the app.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️  DO NOT add `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` for *.pages.dev HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The widely-copied snippet for killing duplicate-content on preview hosts is:
#
#     https://:project.pages.dev/*
#       X-Robots-Tag: noindex
#
# It is WRONG for this project. `ural-travel.pages.dev` is production — the live
# site, and the origin every canonical, hreflang and sitemap URL points at. That
# rule would deindex the entire production site on the next deploy.
#
# It becomes correct ONLY once a custom domain (e.g. uraltravel.com) is attached
# and serving the site, at which point this pages.dev host is the duplicate. If
# that happens, add BOTH of these (versioned hosts are the per-commit previews):
#
#     https://:project.pages.dev/*
#       X-Robots-Tag: noindex
#     https://:version.:project.pages.dev/*
#       X-Robots-Tag: noindex
#
# Commit previews and branch aliases already send `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` by
# default from Cloudflare Pages — verified live on a PR deployment — so nothing
# is needed for them. Keep those hosts crawlable rather than disallowing them in
# robots.txt: a blocked URL is never recrawled, so Google never reads the
# noindex, and already-indexed copies stay in the index. Crawlable + noindex is
# what actually removes a URL.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
